<div id="S6.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluated Taurus using a cycle-accurate simulator across diverse Concrete toolchain workloads.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our two-stage approach combines functionality modeling for computational correctness verification (matching Taurus hardware bit-by-bit) with performance modeling for cycle-accurate timing within seconds.
We use the state-of-the-art Concrete toolchain<span id="footnote11" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">11</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">11</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">11</span>
                
                
                
              Concrete-ML <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> v1.6.1, Commit <span id="footnote11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">8681124</span>. Concrete Compiler <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> v2.7.0, Commit <span id="footnote11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">b7793aeb</span>.</span></span></span> for fair CPU and GPU comparison.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our evaluation spans low-precision workloads (two CNN models with 20 and 50 layers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>) and high-precision workloads including KNN classifier<span id="footnote12" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">12</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">12</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">12</span>
                
                
                
              Scikit-learn based <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>, 3 neighbors, 30 leaves.</span></span></span>, XGBoost regressor<span id="footnote13" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">13</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">13</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">13</span>
                
                
                
              50 estimators, max depth 4, predicting Ames Housing prices <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> with 6-bit quantization.</span></span></span>, decision tree classifier<span id="footnote14" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">14</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">14</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">14</span>
                
                
                
              Scikit-learn based <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>, classifying Bioresponse dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, 18 max depth, 91 nodes, 7-bit quantization.</span></span></span>, and quantized GPT-2 models<span id="footnote15" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">15</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">15</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">15</span>
                
                
                
              HuggingFace pre-trained <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>, 7-bit quantization, 6-bit rounding, single and 12-headed versions.</span></span></span>. Parameter sets are detailed in Table <a href="#S6.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ VI-C1 Real-world Workloads ‣ VI-C Performance Evaluation ‣ VI Evaluation ‣ A Scalable Architecture for Efficient Multi-bit Fully Homomorphic Encryption" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S6.SS3.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">VI-C</span>2 </span>Real-world Workloads Result Analysis</h4>

<div id="S6.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Performance comparisons used an AMD EPYC 7R13 system (48 Zen 3 cores at 3.4 GHz, 256 GB DDR4-3200) with dual NVIDIA RTX A5000 GPUs. The 48 CPU cores match Taurus’s parallel ciphertext processing capacity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS3.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Low bit-width Workloads</span>
CNN models achieve up to <math id="S6.SS3.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="277\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>277</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">277\times</annotation></semantics></math> speedup with single-ciphertext bootstrapping latencies of 0.28 ms (CNN-20) and 0.85 ms (CNN-50).
Compared to Morphling, CNN-20 improves from 0.34 s to 0.0139 s and CNN-50 from 1.72 s to 0.0925 s through compiler optimizations and enhanced polynomial multiplication throughput.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS3.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">High bit-width Workloads</span>
High bit-width workloads show dramatic improvements up to <math id="S6.SS3.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="2595\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2595</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2595\times</annotation></semantics></math> over CPU and <math id="S6.SS3.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1320\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1320</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1320\times</annotation></semantics></math> over GPU, with single-ciphertext bootstrapping latencies ranging from 6.16 ms to 34.67 ms. XGBoost achieved the highest utilization through highly parallel LUT evaluations. This work demonstrates the first homomorphically encrypted GPT-2 inference at usable speeds.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS3.SSS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Utilization and Batchsize</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F15" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.12676v1/utilization.svg" id="S6.F15.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/157;" width="476" height="157"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 15: </span>Taurus Cluster Utilization vs. Workloads</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS3.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Real-world deployments typically process multiple queries in batches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>.
Figure <a href="#S6.F15" title="Fig. 15 ‣ VI-C2 Real-world Workloads Result Analysis ‣ VI-C Performance Evaluation ‣ VI Evaluation ‣ A Scalable Architecture for Efficient Multi-bit Fully Homomorphic Encryption" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a> shows how input batch size affects hardware utilization across different workloads.
For predominantly serial workloads like KNN and Decision Tree, larger batch sizes substantially improve utilization, with KNN reaching 75% utilization at batch size 8.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.SSS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS3.SSS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Observation 7: The parallelism offered by TFHE hardware accelerators can be effectively harnessed in real-world multi-bit TFHE workloads as batch sizes increase.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">VI-D</span> </span><span id="S6.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Quantifying Memory Bandwidth and Architectural Contributions</span></h3>

<figure id="S6.F16" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.12676v1/normalized_speedup.svg" id="S6.F16.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/249;" width="476" height="249"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 16: </span>Normalized Speedup Comparison Across Platforms (Log Scale)</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We measured workload performance on dual AMD EPYC 9654 CPUs with 921.6 GB/s total memory bandwidth, exceeding Taurus’s two HBM stacks (819 GB/s).</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S6.F16" title="Fig. 16 ‣ VI-D Quantifying Memory Bandwidth and Architectural Contributions ‣ VI Evaluation ‣ A Scalable Architecture for Efficient Multi-bit Fully Homomorphic Encryption" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a> compares normalized speedup of dual EPYC 9654 and Taurus relative to EPYC 7R13 (baseline).
The dual 9654 improvement stems from <math id="S6.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="4\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4\times</annotation></semantics></math> more cores (192 vs 48), <math id="S6.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="4.5\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4.5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.5\times</annotation></semantics></math> memory bandwidth increase, up to 13% IPC boost, AVX-512 support, and higher TDP (800 W vs 270 W).
Since dual 9654 provides superior memory bandwidth, the additional Taurus improvement primarily demonstrates microarchitectural contributions.</p>
</div>
</section>
