<div id="S6.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Throughput per unit area comparison.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S6.T3" title="TABLE III ‣ VI-E Comparison with Prior TFHE Accelerators ‣ VI Evaluation ‣ A Scalable Architecture for Efficient Multi-bit Fully Homomorphic Encryption" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a> compares polynomial multiplication throughput with existing ASIC accelerators <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>, with areas scaled to 16 nm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>.<span id="footnote16" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">16</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">16</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">16</span>
              
              
              
            Throughputs measured at <math id="footnote16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math> for typical multi-bit TFHE workloads. Memory controllers and PHY excluded for fair comparison.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Taurus achieves slightly better PolyMult throughput per unit area while supporting much higher polynomial degrees (<math id="S6.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{16}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>16</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{16}</annotation></semantics></math> vs Morphling’s 4096), enabling 10-bit multi-bit TFHE programs compared to the previous 5-bit limitation.
Expanding complex number width from 32 to 48 bits provides plug-and-play compatibility with industrial toolchains like Concrete <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Runtime comparison on benchmarks with XPU-like design.</span></p>
</div>
